From a281f3e4256159104f2ef5c47c3ed4c061781d45 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shapey Review <review@shapey.local>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 08:20:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/13] fix(online): harden the device-clock-skew estimate and pin it
 with tests

Patch 0005 measured serverClockOffset as serverNow - Date.now() per snapshot.
Two defects and one gap:

- Latency/jitter: a snapshot stamped serverNow = S only reaches the handler
  L ms later, so the measurement under-reads the true offset by the jittering
  downstream latency L, and every snapshot moved every displayed deadline by
  the jitter (visible countdown wobble). The new shared estimator
  (src/game/clock-sync.js, used by both the game and the audience page) keeps
  the maximum sample inside a short sliding window - the sample with the
  least latency - so displayed deadlines run at most min(L) late and never
  early, never wobble with jitter, and only ease down slowly under drift.
  Device-clock corrections are adopted on the first snapshot afterwards.
- Study-phase blind spot: the game_started snapshot that launches the scene
  was consumed by the menu and its serverNow never measured, so a skewed
  device showed the whole 15s memorize countdown wrong (0:00 on a fast
  clock) until the first live broadcast. The menu now captures the arrival
  time and the scene measures the offset from the launch snapshot.
- Scene-restart replay: applyOnlineState(state, true) re-measured the offset
  from the replayed snapshot's stale serverNow, under-reading it by the
  snapshot's age. The offset is now updated only for freshly received
  snapshots (receivedAt is captured before JSON.parse in the socket handler);
  replays keep the last measured offset.
- No regression test: tests/clock-sync.test.js pins the estimator's
  behaviour (convergence, jitter immunity, drift easing, device-clock
  corrections, window expiry), and worker/clock-skew-smoke.mjs runs two
  online PvP browsers whose Date.now() is skewed +25s and -25s and verifies
  behaviourally that both display the true turn, study, poll and
  tile-movement timings, agree with each other, do not jump or extend turns
  across repeated snapshots, and hand the turn over at the server-authoritative
  deadline.

No gameplay rules, timings or UI behaviour change: turn switching was and
remains server-authoritative; AI turns remain 5 seconds.
